  <header class="page-head">
    <div>
      <h1 id="title">Balance</h1>
      <div class="sub" id="meta">A proposed owner and cycle for every unscheduled ticket</div>
    </div>
    <div class="bal-actions">
      <button class="pill" id="preview" disabled>Preview</button>
      <button class="pill primary" id="apply" disabled>Apply</button>
    </div>
  </header>
  <form class="bal-controls" id="controls">
    <label>Cycles <input type="number" id="bal-start" min="1" step="1" placeholder="from" /></label>
    <label>to <input type="number" id="bal-end" min="1" step="1" placeholder="to" /></label>
    <label>Points per person <input type="number" id="bal-weekly" min="1" step="1" placeholder="auto" /></label>
    <label>Max lead cycles <input type="number" id="bal-lead" min="0" step="1" placeholder="none" /></label>
    <button class="pill" id="recompute" type="submit">Recompute</button>
  </form>
  <div id="page" class="page-body">
    <p class="empty">Loading…</p>
  </div>
  <div id="out" class="bal-out" hidden></div>
  <details class="legend">
    <summary>How to read this proposal</summary>
    <div class="legend-body">
      <p>
        Balance takes every open ticket with no cycle, orders them blockers first and then by priority,
        milestone target, and size, and gives each one the best-fitting owner in the earliest cycle that
        still has room. A person's room in a cycle is their own velocity, deflated for an on-call week
        and for days out of office, minus what they are already committed to. A dependency chain stays
        with one owner so it does not hand off mid-flight.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is greedy rather than optimal, so read it as a starting point to edit rather than an answer.
        Untick anything you disagree with before previewing. Work that does not fit inside the cycle
        window comes back with an owner and no cycle, so the decision is kept without pretending there
        is room for it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Preview is a dry run against the same write path the editor uses: it reports what each op would
        change without touching Linear. Nothing is written until you press Apply.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
